# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config, so on a Windows
# machine the default is to rewrite line endings on checkout. For most of a repo
# that is harmless. For the files below it is not:
#
#   * a shell script checked out with CRLF fails at the shebang — `sh` reads the
#     trailing \r as part of the interpreter path, and the error names a file
#     that plainly exists.
#   * a Makefile is whitespace-significant; the same scripts are also COPYed
#     into Linux images, where \r never becomes acceptable.
#
# Pinning eol=lf here means these files are byte-identical on every platform no
# matter what a given developer's core.autocrlf happens to be — the repo stops
# depending on per-machine Git configuration. See docs/26-windows.md.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# Windows-only helpers are the exception: cmd.exe requires CRLF in batch files.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
